# Repository line-ending policy.
#
# The index stores LF for every text file (verified: `git ls-files --eol` showed
# i/lf across the tree), but without this file the *working tree* shape was left
# to each clone: this machine has core.autocrlf=true, which materializes CRLF on
# checkout and, worse, lets a tool that writes CRLF (a PowerShell pipeline, an
# editor default) slip CR into a file whose committed form is LF. The encoding
# gate then reports CR warnings that are really checkout/tool artefacts.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` fixes both directions at the repository level:
#   - `text=auto`  -> git decides text vs binary by content (a NUL in the first
#                     8 KiB means binary), normalizing text to LF in the index;
#   - `eol=lf`     -> checkout writes LF, and it WINS over core.autocrlf, so the
#                     working tree is LF regardless of the machine's git config.
#
# `binary` is kept explicit as documentation: the auto-detection above is what
# actually decides (a file matching `binary` is never normalized, diffed as text
# or merged as text), and naming it makes the intent obvious to the next reader.
# Screenshots and CSS art belong here too, so a future committed image cannot be
# mangled by a stray eol rule.
* text=auto eol=lf
# Generated bundle output is not tracked (see .gitignore); excluded explicitly so
# a future `git add -f lib` can never reintroduce a mixed-endings blob.
/lib/** -text
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.pdf binary
